{"qid":"Q27260","names":["Sind-Samma Dynasty"],"title":"Sind-Samma Dynasty","endonym":{"native":"سما راڄ","lang":"sd"},"capsule":"The Samma dynasty (سما راڄ) ruled Sindh and parts of the lower Indus valley from their capital at Thatta from c. 1335, displacing the Sumra and establishing one of the most sophisticated regional courts of late medieval India. Their most celebrated ruler, Jam Nizamuddin II 'Nindo' (r. c. 1461–1508), patronized the poet Shah Abdul Latif Bhittai's antecedents and oversaw a period of architectural achievement at Makli Hill, whose necropolis remains one of the largest in Asia. The Samma were overthrown by Arghun ruler Shah Beg Arghun in 1521, who seized Thatta and ended their independent rule, though the dataset records attestation through 1526.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The Samma realm in Sindh is attested by local chronicles and the Makli necropolis epigraphy, but precise northern and eastern extents remain uncertain due to limited regional historiography.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Samma dynasty","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Samma-dynasty"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Samma dynasty","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samma_dynasty"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Makli","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Makli"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Samma dynasty","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Jam Unar I","nativeName":"","roman":"","start":1351,"end":1352,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Jam Banbinah I","nativeName":"","roman":"","start":1352,"end":1367,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"q